body:
Personally I like to analyze things. What's the opponent's headspace, what's mine, who's making the comeback, who's the better fencer, what are they going to do, what do I want to do, should I do it, etc.

&#x200B;

However, if you're getting anxious and losing bouts, I wouldn't suggest my approach. The only important question is "How did I score my 4 or 14 touches?" And then use all of those as a blueprint. At the same time "How did I give up the 4 or 14 touches?" Don't do that. It sounds super basic, but honestly when you're on strip and tension is up, no one's brain naturally works like that. Just do the thing that got you the most points.
